# Decision records

Every decision for **{{PROJECT_NAME}}** is one file in **`docs/decisions/`**, indexed in
[docs/decisions/README.md](../docs/decisions/README.md). One folder, one number
sequence, whatever layer the decision concerns — the index's `Scope` column narrows
them.

This matches the default location and format of the `tsh-core` plugin, so
`/tsh-core:managing-decision-records` writes a conforming record and updates the index
without any configuration. Without that plugin, follow this file by hand.

## Why one folder

- **One sequence.** Records split across folders each restart at 001, so three
  different records answer to "zero zero one" in the same conversation.
- **One place to look.** A reader asking "was this already decided?" checks a single
  index, not a folder per layer.
- **Scope is a column, not a directory.** A decision often touches two layers; a
  folder forces a false choice, a column does not.

## Files and the index

- Filename: `0001-short-kebab-title.md`, zero-padded, sequential, never reused and
  never renumbered. The number is how other records refer to this one.
- The title states the resolution ("Serve images from a CDN"), not the topic.
- Every record has a row in the index, added in the same change. An unindexed record
  does not exist.
- The index is an index, never a copy: path, scope, tags, one-line description, status.

| Column | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| Path | Relative link to the record |
| Scope | `global`, or the layer it belongs to: `backend`, `frontend`, `design`, `mobile`, `platform`, `product`, `delivery`, `quality` |
| Tags | Comma-separated topics, in the words a task would use |
| Description | One line: what was decided, not why |
| Status | One of the five below |

`Scope: global` means the decision binds every layer. Use a layer value only when a
reader working elsewhere can safely ignore it.

## Where the record is written, and by what

The archive is **`docs/decisions/` in this repository**, always. A decision discussed
while working in a code repository is still written here — that is what "one folder,
one sequence" means, and it is the single easiest rule to break, because the tool
writing the record defaults to the repository the session started in.

| You are working in | The archive path is |
| --- | --- |
| this repository | `docs/decisions/` |
| the project catalog | `{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context/docs/decisions/` |
| a code repository beside it | `../{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context/docs/decisions/` |

Use **`/tsh-core:managing-decision-records`** and give it that path as the archive
location. That skill owns the record format, the status vocabulary, numbering,
superseding and the index sync; this file exists to say **where** the records live and
**what the `Scope` column means**, not to restate the format. Where the two disagree,
the skill wins and this file is corrected.

Without that plugin installed, follow the template at the end of this file by hand and
update [docs/decisions/README.md](../docs/decisions/README.md) in the same change.

**A code repository's own archive is a different thing.** A record about that
repository's internal conventions may live there. Anything another repository or
another role would need to know belongs here.

## Statuses

Exactly five. Only `Accepted` binds; every other status is history — read it to see
what was considered, never apply it as a current constraint.

| Status | Means |
| --- | --- |
| `Proposed` | Written, not yet agreed. Nothing is built on it. |
| `Accepted` | Agreed by whoever owns the decision, and in force. |
| `Superseded` | Was in force; replaced by a later record, which is named. |
| `Rejected` | Considered and not adopted. |
| `Deprecated` | Was in force; no longer applies, with no replacement. |

Transitions: `Proposed → Accepted | Rejected`, `Accepted → Superseded | Deprecated`.
Nothing else. Reviving a superseded or rejected decision means a new record that cites
the old one, never an edit backwards.

A record's body is frozen once `Accepted`; after that only the status line and
`Superseded by` change. A `Superseded` record keeps its body, including the parts now
known to be wrong — it is a historical document.

**Nobody sets a status alone.** Write `Proposed`, say which status you believe is
correct and who confirms it, and leave it to them.

**Non-binding rows stay in the index.** A `Rejected` row is what answers the next
person proposing the same thing.

## Template

```markdown
# 0001: <Title stating the resolution>

- **Status:** Proposed · **Date:** {{DATE}} · **Decided by:** <who>
- **Scope:** global · **Tags:** <comma-separated>
- **Supersedes:** — · **Superseded by:** —

## Decision

<The choice, first sentence, present tense, as settled fact.>

## Context

<The constraint that forced it.>

## Alternatives

| Option | Why it lost |
| --- | --- |

## Consequences

<What gets easier, what gets harder, how reversible it is.>
```

When the rationale cannot be recovered, say so in the record. An invented rationale is
worse than an admitted gap, because it gets cited later as if it were true.

## What is not a decision record

A convention — "we use two-space indentation", "handlers live in this folder" — belongs
in the owning workspace's `CLAUDE.md`, not here. A record is for a choice with
alternatives that someone might reopen.
